@sagearc Absolutely — from the downstream side, we’d really like to see this kept alive and landed.
The pilot showed that the CPU-native simulator gives us a lean, deterministic substrate for KV / scheduler regression work without requiring full model execution. If you're willing to resolve the current conflicts and the MoE prepack gap, we’ll actively use and build on top of this path.
@njhill — Sage mentioned he can resolve the conflicts / gaps if there’s core interest in getting #47922 landed. From our pilot, having this CPU-native simulator in `main` looks valuable well beyond a one-off test.
One immediate follow-up we’d like to try is a **paired MRV1 ↔ MRV2 KV-behavior regression** using the same canonical request trace and evidence contract: same requests, same KV budget, same prescribed token stream, then identifying the first request boundary where observable cache behavior stops agreeing.
#47922 would give us a strong MRV2-side substrate for this without requiring full model execution. If that kind of migration / regression evidence would be useful to core, @LioEinaudi and I would be happy to prototype the paired run next.

@sagearc we would like to see this land and have been building on it. I opened https://github.com/sagearc/vllm/pull/6 against your `kv-cache-simulator` branch with three things that might save you the rebase work:

- merge of `main` at `6a70913590` (only the `base_loader.load_model` conflict);
- the simulated worker running on CUDA-alike hosts as well as CPU: the model stays on the meta device and no KV tensors are allocated, but the runner's buffers live on a real device so the stock Triton kernels are used and `triton-cpu` is not required. This also avoids the MoE `prepack_moe_weight` meta-kernel failure @ZC502 hit on the CPU build: on an H200 all three test models (incl. `gpt-oss-120b` and `Qwen3-235B-A22B`) pass;
- the `execute_model()`/`sample_tokens()` output contract the real runner uses, so `--simulate-forward` no longer has to force `async_scheduling=False` and works under the default `step_with_batch_queue` path (verified with `vllm serve` + `vllm bench serve`, multiprocess engine core).

Feel free to take the commits as you see fit (or just the merge). Separately, we have a timing model for the simulated steps (a per-step `TimingProvider`, so TTFT/TPOT come out realistic) stacked on this; we will propose it as an RFC once this PR is in, since it adds a config surface.

